WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:19.630
CONSTANT CONCESSIONS:
I’m sure you know about Microsoft’s successor

00:00:19.630 --> 00:00:22.710
to Internet Explorer: Microsoft Edge.

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:27.500
But, did you know that Edge is actually powered
by Google.

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:32.669
And it’s not some sort of obscure connection
either, the entirety of Edge is built on Chromium

00:00:32.669 --> 00:00:35.940
which is the open source version of Chrome.

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:40.789
When Google released Chromium, they were primarily
aiming to help small developers and to support

00:00:40.789 --> 00:00:42.620
the open source community.

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:48.440
But, somehow, their biggest competitor has
ended up adopting it themselves.

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:51.010
And this isn’t a temporary solution either.

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:54.950
In fact, Microsoft has already tried to compete
against Google.

00:00:54.950 --> 00:01:00.059
They of course had Internet explorer, but
the original verison of Edge was also built

00:01:00.059 --> 00:01:05.580
on Microsoft’s proprietary engine, EdgeHTML,
and their Chakra Javascript engine.

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:11.650
But, this version of Edge got destroyed so
badly in the marketplace that Microsoft finally

00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:16.320
threw in the towel and announced that they
too would switch to Chrome.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:21.880
Since then, Microsoft has even gone as far
as discontinuing the legacy version of Edge,

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:24.530
and that itself was 2 years ago.

00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:28.970
So, it doesn’t seem like Microsoft will
be returning to the browser engine space anytime

00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:29.970
soon.

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:33.110
It’s not just the browser space that they
conceded on either.

00:01:33.110 --> 00:01:36.650
They also gave in on the backend side of things.

00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:42.840
Back in 2015, Microsoft ditched Windows and
started using Linux for their backend infrastructure.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:48.969
Since then, Microsoft has even launched their
own version of Linux.

00:01:48.969 --> 00:01:54.960
These two are likely Microsoft’s most notable
concessions, but this just scratches the surface.

00:01:54.960 --> 00:02:00.670
They also conceded in the smartphone space,
the music streaming space, the Windows 8 space,

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:05.219
the smart assistant space, and now, even the
AI space.

00:02:05.219 --> 00:02:10.220
Instead of trying to push an in-house version
of ChatGPT, Microsoft has decided to embrace

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:13.000
ChatGPT itself from the getgo.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:19.129
Clearly, Microsoft has done a lot of conceding
and losing over the past decade, so is this

00:02:19.129 --> 00:02:21.860
the end of Microsoft’s mega monopoly?

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:26.450
Are they going to end up as the next Cisco
or Intel?

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:29.459
The answer is absolutely not.

00:02:29.459 --> 00:02:35.310
Microsoft has been doing a lot of public conceding,
but internally, they are the strongest tech

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:39.220
company in the world, and here’s why.

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:44.380
HARD HEADED:
While Microsoft’s approach may seem weak

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:49.620
and pathetic at first glance, they’re actually
making all of the right choices.

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:55.050
Here’s the thing, over the past 20 to 30
years, companies have gotten into this mindset

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:58.720
that they have to be present in every aspect
of your lives.

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:00.360
Do you need to wake up early?

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:04.290
You should be using an Apple Alarm on your
Apple iPhone.

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:05.629
Do you need to workout?

00:03:05.629 --> 00:03:10.540
You should be using an Apple Watch to track
your workout, and Airpods to power your workout.

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:12.400
Do you need to text your friends?

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:13.850
You should be using iMessage.

00:03:13.850 --> 00:03:15.319
Do you need to video call your family?

00:03:15.319 --> 00:03:16.959
You should be using Facetime.

00:03:16.959 --> 00:03:18.870
Do you need to just sleep?

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:22.319
Well, you should be using an Apple Watch to
track your sleep.

00:03:22.319 --> 00:03:25.830
This trend is not just apparent on the front
end either.

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:29.830
These companies want to control the entire
backend process as well.

00:03:29.830 --> 00:03:33.530
If you’re building a service, you should
be using Google’s servers, Google’s compute

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:38.010
engines, Google’s virtual machines, Google’s
cloud functions, Google’s databases, Google’s

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:43.739
clouds NATs, Google’s VPC networks, and
all of this needs to go through Google Fiber.

00:03:43.739 --> 00:03:49.580
Basically, if a product or service has any
association with tech at all, these companies

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:51.069
want to a part of it.

00:03:51.069 --> 00:03:53.160
And you can’t really blame them.

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:58.069
They want to constantly grow bigger and bigger,
so they’re trying to control every industry

00:03:58.069 --> 00:04:01.079
they can think of from semi conductor design
to thermostats.

00:04:01.079 --> 00:04:04.430
But, here’s the ironic part of it.

00:04:04.430 --> 00:04:09.220
To truly continue growing indefinitely, the
key is not isolation.

00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:16.199
The key is collaboration, and this is precisely
what Microsoft has come to realize.

00:04:16.199 --> 00:04:21.549
You see, it wasn’t that long ago that Microsoft
also had this same mindset of conquering everything.

00:04:21.549 --> 00:04:28.030
By 2000, they had hit 2 products out of the
park: Windows and Microsoft Office.

00:04:28.030 --> 00:04:33.289
These products revolutionized not just the
computer space or even the tech industry.

00:04:33.289 --> 00:04:38.910
These products revolutionized the entire world
permanently, and this gave Microsoft such

00:04:38.910 --> 00:04:40.449
a massive ego.

00:04:40.449 --> 00:04:46.260
They felt that they could conquer anything
and everything, and to some degree this was

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:47.260
true.

00:04:47.260 --> 00:04:52.039
They could put out crappy products like Internet
Explorer and end up controlling 95% of the

00:04:52.039 --> 00:04:55.600
market simply because of their influence and
control.

00:04:55.600 --> 00:05:00.539
This false sense of superiority led them to
the email space, the search space, the messaging

00:05:00.539 --> 00:05:05.270
space, the video conferencing space, the gaming
space, the music space, and who knows what

00:05:05.270 --> 00:05:06.270
else.

00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:09.530
But, eventually, Microsoft reached an impasse.

00:05:09.530 --> 00:05:14.250
They kept throwing crap at the wall but nothing
was sticking.

00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:18.540
In fact, the crap that stuck earlier was starting
to fall down as well.

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:24.789
Chrome was destroying Internet Explorer, Gmail
was obliterating Hotmail, the iPod was humiliating

00:05:24.789 --> 00:05:30.600
the Zune, the playstation was killing the
Xbox, and MSN Messenger, well that was going

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:31.990
full on extinct.

00:05:31.990 --> 00:05:37.130
For the first time in their history, Microsoft
felt that people didn’t actually want their

00:05:37.130 --> 00:05:38.320
new products.

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:41.040
But, maybe, that was just bad luck.

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:42.630
So, they continued pushing.

00:05:42.630 --> 00:05:48.539
They bought Skype, they launched Bing, and
they entered the smartphone space, but once

00:05:48.539 --> 00:05:50.539
again, they got annihiliated.

00:05:50.539 --> 00:05:57.569
Their stock had stagnated for 17 years, their
CEO, while a nice guy, was also a meme and

00:05:57.569 --> 00:06:02.830
hated by shareholders, and public sentiment
against Microsoft was worse than ever.

00:06:02.830 --> 00:06:07.000
Microsoft needed change and they needed it
desperately.

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:09.699
Enter Satya Nadella.

00:06:09.699 --> 00:06:14.460
BE HUMBLE:
If you were asked if you want to be the CEO

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:16.110
of Microsoft, what would you say?

00:06:16.110 --> 00:06:17.110
For those of you who want the job, you’d
probably be selling your butt off.

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:21.389
Even those of you who don’t want the job
would probably be selling you butt off.

00:06:21.389 --> 00:06:25.770
After all, you only need this job for like
60 days and you’d be set for life.

00:06:25.770 --> 00:06:29.860
But, Satya didn’t even try to sell himself
to the board.

00:06:29.860 --> 00:06:35.180
When they asked him if he wanted to be CEO,
he replied quote “Only if you want me to

00:06:35.180 --> 00:06:36.520
be CEO.”

00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:41.569
Most boards would decipher this as a lack
of enthusiasm and move onto the next candidate,

00:06:41.569 --> 00:06:47.090
but somehow, Satya would end up getting the
job, and he would spread this humble mentality

00:06:47.090 --> 00:06:48.889
across all of Microsoft.

00:06:48.889 --> 00:06:53.669
His first order of business was scrapping
off all of the crap on the floor.

00:06:53.669 --> 00:06:58.129
He shutdown dozens of sectors like their smartphone
business, he replaced the worst OS of all

00:06:58.129 --> 00:07:02.449
time, Windows 8, and he replaced Internet
Explorer with Edge.

00:07:02.449 --> 00:07:06.199
But, all of this was to just cut the fat on
the consumer side.

00:07:06.199 --> 00:07:10.490
Really, he didn’t even want to pursue the
consumer side.

00:07:10.490 --> 00:07:16.479
The reality was that Google, Facebook, Apple,
and Amazon had destroyed them in this market,

00:07:16.479 --> 00:07:22.069
and unlike Microsoft, people actually liked
these companies at least at the time.

00:07:22.069 --> 00:07:25.990
So, Satya decided to give consumers a break
from Microsoft.

00:07:25.990 --> 00:07:31.889
He basically made Windows and Office free
for end users and got out of their way.

00:07:31.889 --> 00:07:36.130
In the meantime, Satya decided to attack an
up and coming market: cloud infrastructure.

00:07:36.130 --> 00:07:42.650
At the time, AWS was really the only player
on the market, and while they were excellent,

00:07:42.650 --> 00:07:45.099
they were also as expensive as iPhones.

00:07:45.099 --> 00:07:50.200
So, Satya decided to focus his efforts on
the budget cloud market.

00:07:50.200 --> 00:07:54.669
And today, Azure blows AWS out of the water
in terms of cost.

00:07:54.669 --> 00:07:58.669
In fact, Azure is up to 5 times cheaper than
AWS.

00:07:58.669 --> 00:08:04.270
Satya also put aside Microsoft’s pride and
started offering Linux options because that’s

00:08:04.270 --> 00:08:06.639
what enterprise customers wanted.

00:08:06.639 --> 00:08:10.990
For many companies, this was simply an offer
they couldn’t refuse.

00:08:10.990 --> 00:08:15.389
A cloud platform from a reputable company
that ran Linux and was substantially cheaper

00:08:15.389 --> 00:08:18.199
than AWS was a no brainer.

00:08:18.199 --> 00:08:23.360
But, the cloud was just the beginning, Satya
was intent on tailoring Microsoft’s products

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:24.710
to the needs of enterprises.

00:08:24.710 --> 00:08:29.349
A great example of this is Microsoft’s acquisition
of LinkedIn.

00:08:29.349 --> 00:08:34.029
LinkedIn has become the go to place for recruiters
and hiring managers.

00:08:34.029 --> 00:08:38.599
Satya also made the decision to ditch Skype
and build a new video conferencing solution

00:08:38.599 --> 00:08:41.590
just for enterprises: Microsoft Teams.

00:08:41.590 --> 00:08:45.310
And today, Teams is looking to overtake Zoom.

00:08:45.310 --> 00:08:48.830
At this point, Microsoft is no longer a consumer
business.

00:08:48.830 --> 00:08:54.640
In fact, consumer based revenue accounts for
the smallest portion of Microsoft’s revenue.

00:08:54.640 --> 00:08:59.830
Last quarter, for example, Microsoft made
$16.5 billion from Productivity and Business

00:08:59.830 --> 00:09:06.060
Processes, $20.3 billion from Intelligent
Cloud, and only $13.3 billion from Personal

00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:07.060
Computing.

00:09:07.060 --> 00:09:11.170
And that includes Windows, Bing, Surface Tablets,
and the Xbox.

00:09:11.170 --> 00:09:16.820
In other words, Microsoft can shut down their
consumer business and they would still be

00:09:16.820 --> 00:09:17.959
a trillion dollar company.

00:09:17.959 --> 00:09:23.130
But, that doesn’t mean that Microsoft is
giving up on or exiting the consumer space

00:09:23.130 --> 00:09:24.290
by any means.

00:09:24.290 --> 00:09:28.040
In fact, it’s quite the opposite.

00:09:28.040 --> 00:09:33.519
COMEBACK:
Now that Microsoft is no longer reliant on

00:09:33.519 --> 00:09:39.910
revenue from consumers, they can hit the consumer
space harder than ever before, but Microsoft

00:09:39.910 --> 00:09:42.290
can’t just revert to their old strategy
though.

00:09:42.290 --> 00:09:47.040
If they do, it won’t be long before they’re
in the same position that they were in 10

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:48.040
years ago.

00:09:48.040 --> 00:09:54.330
See, the key isn’t to try to replicate the
success of the iPhone or Chrome or Facebook.

00:09:54.330 --> 00:09:57.990
The key is to build upon these hits and make
them better.

00:09:57.990 --> 00:10:02.660
In fact, you could argue that this is what
made these products themselves successful

00:10:02.660 --> 00:10:04.010
in the first place.

00:10:04.010 --> 00:10:09.589
The iPhone made cell phones better, Chrome
made browsers better, and Facebook made social

00:10:09.589 --> 00:10:10.880
media better.

00:10:10.880 --> 00:10:15.200
The problem with companies trying to expand
to everything by themselves is that they rarely

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:18.140
make said industries better.

00:10:18.140 --> 00:10:23.100
The Zune did not make mp3 players better and
the Windows phone did not make smartphones

00:10:23.100 --> 00:10:24.100
better.

00:10:24.100 --> 00:10:28.600
These products were simply alternatives that
companies assumed that people would buy just

00:10:28.600 --> 00:10:32.920
because it’s associated with a given brand
and ecosystem.

00:10:32.920 --> 00:10:37.339
This is basically the definition of what Google
and Apple are doing right now.

00:10:37.339 --> 00:10:43.160
But, as Microsoft found, this strategy doesn’t
work forever, and that’s why they’re not

00:10:43.160 --> 00:10:45.240
making the same mistake again.

00:10:45.240 --> 00:10:50.139
Instead of trying to create yet another browser
and convincing developers and users to switch,

00:10:50.139 --> 00:10:53.190
Microsoft embraced Chrome and addressed its
painpoints.

00:10:53.190 --> 00:10:58.019
They made the browser more efficient and slashed
its atrocious ram usage.

00:10:58.019 --> 00:11:06.130
With 60 tabs open, Chrome gobbles up 3.7 GB
of ram and FireFox gobbles up 3.9 GB of ram,

00:11:06.130 --> 00:11:09.519
but Edge only uses 2.9 GB.

00:11:09.519 --> 00:11:13.279
Microsoft isn’t hell bent on trying get
you to use Bing either.

00:11:13.279 --> 00:11:17.660
You can switch your default search engine
to Google within a matter of 7 seconds.

00:11:17.660 --> 00:11:22.180
You also get the power of the Chrome webstore
while not giving up the power of Microsoft’s

00:11:22.180 --> 00:11:27.850
classic products like the Office suite and
Outlook, so you get the best of both worlds.

00:11:27.850 --> 00:11:31.870
Microsoft is using this same approach when
it comes to Bing as well.

00:11:31.870 --> 00:11:36.399
Instead of trying to force Cortana down people’s
throats, they decided to partner with something

00:11:36.399 --> 00:11:39.660
that’s already working: ChatGPT.

00:11:39.660 --> 00:11:44.240
And by the looks of it, people are excited
for a Microsoft product for the first time

00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:46.300
in a long time.

00:11:46.300 --> 00:11:52.439
The new Bing was able to get 1 million people
onto the waiting list within just 48 hours.

00:11:52.439 --> 00:11:56.649
And it’s just a matter of time until these
collaborative moves start hitting the bottom

00:11:56.649 --> 00:11:57.649
line.

00:11:57.649 --> 00:12:02.850
At the end of 2021, Microsoft was able to
overtake Apple and become the world’s largest

00:12:02.850 --> 00:12:09.209
company, but you probably didn’t even hear
about this because Microsoft didn’t do anything

00:12:09.209 --> 00:12:11.040
particularly cool.

00:12:11.040 --> 00:12:17.940
And I think this is really the story of the
new Microsoft: the background juggernaut.

00:12:17.940 --> 00:12:22.779
TIME IN THE MARKET
In the end, Microsoft is no longer trying

00:12:22.779 --> 00:12:28.730
to constantly score winners product after
product because that’s a losers game.

00:12:28.730 --> 00:12:33.880
Consumer sentiment changes all the time, and
it’s impossible to always be the most desirable

00:12:33.880 --> 00:12:36.579
company with the most desirable products.

00:12:36.579 --> 00:12:41.790
But, what is sustainable though is associating
yourself with up and coming winners and just

00:12:41.790 --> 00:12:45.120
trying to get a small portion of that pie
for yourself.

00:12:45.120 --> 00:12:50.529
This strategy will never yield in slam dunks
because the main product whether it be ChatGPT

00:12:50.529 --> 00:12:54.670
or Linux will always outshine what you’re
doing.

00:12:54.670 --> 00:13:01.070
But, at the same time, you also won’t end
up like Yahoo or Aol.

00:13:01.070 --> 00:13:05.970
If ChatGPT’s time in the limelight is over,
no worries, you have hundreds of other winners

00:13:05.970 --> 00:13:07.670
that you also bet on.

00:13:07.670 --> 00:13:13.660
There’s a saying in the stock market: Time
in the market beats timing the market.

00:13:13.660 --> 00:13:16.579
Most companies are trying to time the market.

00:13:16.579 --> 00:13:22.060
Meta, for example is trying to hit the next
Gamestop short squeeze with the Metaverse.

00:13:22.060 --> 00:13:26.550
Apple is busy chasing after the AR, VR, and
EV markets.

00:13:26.550 --> 00:13:31.250
And Google thinks that they can hold their
ground against thousands of new AI options.

00:13:31.250 --> 00:13:35.560
Meanwhile, Microsoft is just investing into
the S&amp;P 500.

00:13:35.560 --> 00:13:41.639
It’s not flashy or particularly interesting,
but over the long term, not even Warren Buffett

00:13:41.639 --> 00:13:44.019
is able to hold up against it.

00:13:44.019 --> 00:13:48.790
And that’s why I think Microsoft is the
strongest tech company in the world.

00:13:48.790 --> 00:13:54.009
Flashy products from Meta and Apple may excite
the press, but in terms of long term sustainable

00:13:54.009 --> 00:13:56.959
growth, Microsoft is the real winners.

00:13:56.959 --> 00:13:59.050
But, that’s just what I think.

00:13:59.050 --> 00:14:01.690
Did you realize just how well Microsoft was
doing?

00:14:01.690 --> 00:14:03.009
Comment that down below.

00:14:03.009 --> 00:14:08.490
Also, drop a like if you wish more companies
gave up their ego, at least partly.

00:14:08.490 --> 00:14:12.579
And of course, consider checking out our discord
community to suggest future video ideas and

00:14:12.579 --> 00:14:26.529
consider subscribing to see more questions
